---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CropPdf from '@/components/tools/pdf/CropPdf.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="crop-pdf">
  <CropPdf client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>How to crop a PDF</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Drop the PDF above. The first page renders immediately and becomes the canvas you drag on.</li>
        <li>
          Drag the blue rectangle to the area you want to keep, or press
          <strong>Auto-detect margins</strong> to fit it to the ink on the page.
        </li>
        <li>
          To use numbers, type the trim in millimetres for each edge. The two controls stay in sync, so
          you can use the whole tool from the keyboard.
        </li>
        <li>
          Step through pages to check the crop for the rest of the document, choose if it applies to
          all, odd, even or a range of pages, then press <strong>Crop PDF</strong>.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Cropping hides content; it does not delete it</h2>
      <p>
        This is the most misunderstood fact about PDF cropping. A PDF page has several rectangles. The
        <strong>MediaBox</strong> is the sheet on which the page was defined. The
        <strong>CropBox</strong> is the window that a reader shows. Cropping makes those rectangles
        smaller; all drawing instructions outside the window stay in the file, outside the frame.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is correct for trimming scanner shadows or the grey gutter of a photocopy. It is wrong if
        the material outside the box is confidential, because anyone can make the boxes wider again and
        see it. For that case you need redaction, which rasterises the page and destroys the underlying
        objects.
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Page box</th>
            <th scope="col">What it means</th>
            <th scope="col">Who reads it</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>MediaBox</td>
            <td>The physical sheet the page is imposed on. Required on every page.</td>
            <td>Everything, including old printer RIPs</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>CropBox</td>
            <td>The region a viewer shows and a printer images. Defaults to the MediaBox.</td>
            <td>Desktop PDF readers, browsers, most print drivers</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>BleedBox / TrimBox / ArtBox</td>
            <td>Prepress hints for finished size and bleed. Ignored on screen.</td>
            <td>Commercial print workflows</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        The <strong>Shrink the MediaBox too</strong> switch is on by default. A crop of only the CropBox
        looks correct in a desktop reader but prints at full size on a device that reads only the
        MediaBox. Turn the switch off to keep the original sheet size in the file.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Why one crop works on pages of different sizes</h2>
      <p>
        The rectangle you draw is stored as a proportion of the page (for example 6% in from the left
        and 4% down from the top), not as a fixed measurement. When the crop is applied, the tool
        measures each page and converts the proportion into the points of that page. A report with A4
        body pages and A3 fold-out tables thus gets a consistent trim, not a fixed 20 mm that is too
        much for the small pages and too little for the large ones.
      </p>
      <p>
        Rotated pages work the same way. If a page has a 90° rotation flag, the rectangle you drew on
        screen is mapped back through that rotation before the boxes are written, so the crop is where
        you saw it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Common reasons to crop</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Reading on a small screen: a trim of 25 mm from each side of an academic paper makes the text
          about 15% larger on a tablet, with no zoom.
        </li>
        <li>
          Scanner artefacts: a flatbed scan of a smaller original leaves a black or grey border.
          Auto-detect finds the paper edge and removes it in one step.
        </li>
        <li>
          Slide decks: exported slides are often inside a large canvas. Crop to the slide area to make
          them fill the screen in a PDF reader.
        </li>
        <li>
          Removing a header or footer band: trim only from the top or bottom. The content is hidden from
          the printed output, but the text layer is still searchable.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Nothing is uploaded</h2>
      <p>
        pdf.js renders the page preview in a web worker in this tab, and pdf-lib writes the crop in the
        same tab. This product has no server that could receive the file. Load the page, turn off your
        network, and the tool still works.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
